  <h2>What goes on a painting estimate</h2>
  <p>
    A painting estimate has to show the customer exactly what gets painted and what it costs. A
    homeowner comparing three painters lines up the job specs first: paintable area, which surfaces,
    how many coats, the paint and sheen, and the colors. Two estimates at very different prices are
    often two different jobs, one coat against two or trim left out, and the specs strip under the
    header makes that visible. The line items are grouped the way a painter works the job.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Prep and protection:</strong> moving and covering furniture, masking, patching and sanding,
      caulking trim, spot-priming, and an allowance for drywall repair beyond normal patching.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Walls and ceilings:</strong> coats and sheen by surface, plus access for anything taller
      than a standard ladder reaches, such as a two-story foyer or stairwell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trim, doors and other:</strong> baseboard, doors, window casings, and the final walkthrough
      and touch-up.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Anything the customer might add, such as a stair rail or crown molding, goes in the
    optional-items table, priced but outside the total.
  </p>

  <h2>How to measure rooms for a paint estimate</h2>
  <p>
    Measure each room’s length, width and wall height. Wall area is the perimeter times the height,
    2 × (L + W) × H, less the openings. The template deducts 21 sq ft for each door and 15 sq ft for
    each window, and both sizes are editable. Ceiling area is length times width. Baseboard runs the
    perimeter, less the width of each door. Three rooms from the completed example:
  </p>
