  <p>
    Gallons are area × coats ÷ spread rate. Take the spread rate from the can and use the low end on
    rough or porous walls. The Paint Takeoff sheet totals gallons by product and by wall color,
    because paint is bought by color: in the example, three colors rounded up separately come to 16
    gallons of wall paint where the job total alone says 15. Labor hours are quantity × coats ÷ your
    crew’s production rate, for prep and for paint, so the estimate carries the hours the job will
    actually take.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing a repaint line by line</h2>
  <p>
    Price walls and ceilings by the square foot, baseboard by the linear foot, and doors and windows
    by the each. A per-room price hides the difference between a powder room and a two-story foyer,
    and a per-square-foot price shows it. Put high-wall access on its own line, because planks and
    extension ladders are hours a flat rate does not cover. Build every unit price from cost on the
    Unit Price Builder: paint per unit, plus labor hours at your fully loaded rate, plus tape,
    plastic and caulk, then overhead, then profit as a
    <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">margin on price rather than a markup</a>. A 15%
    markup on cost is only a 13% margin.
  </p>
  <p>
    The completed example is a main-floor repaint of an occupied two-story in Henrico, Virginia: six
    rooms, 2,547 sq ft of walls at two coats, 877 sq ft of ceilings at one coat, 251 LF of
    baseboard, 7 doors and 10 windows. Prep and protection come to $1,825.40, walls and ceilings to
    $4,842.45, and trim and doors to $2,457.50. That is $9,125.35 in total, about $2.67 per square
    foot of paintable area. Sales tax shows as included because the painter pays tax on the paint
    and builds it into the prices. The 25% deposit is $2,281.34. The takeoff puts the job at 91.7
    labor hours, about six working days for a two-person crew.
  </p>

  <h2>Drywall repair, color changes and change orders</h2>
  <p>
    Prep is where interior repaints run over. You can count nail holes on a walkthrough, but you
    cannot see how far a crack opens once it is scraped, or what is behind a mirror that comes down.
    Write that uncertainty into the estimate. Include a fixed number of drywall repair hours, state
    the hourly rate beyond them, and promise photos. Colors are the other argument. Put the rule in
    the terms: colors are final before work starts, and a change after paint goes on, or a deep
    color that needs a third coat, is priced separately. Anything added mid-job goes on a
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a>, signed before the work is done. When the
    job is finished, bill the balance on an <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">invoice</a> that
    references the estimate number and lists the approved changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimate, quote or proposal?</h2>
  <p>
    For most residential repaints, this customer-facing estimate is the whole sale: a price, the
    terms and a signature. If the customer wants a fixed price with less line detail, a
    <a href="/templates/construction-quote/">construction quote</a> is the shorter form. On a commercial
    repaint bid to a GC or property manager against drawings and a finish schedule, build your cost on
    the <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate worksheet</a>
    and send a <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> with inclusions, exclusions
    and alternates instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on painting estimates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pricing floor area instead of wall area. The example’s foyer has 96 sq ft of floor and 642 sq
      ft of wall.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leaving the coats unstated. One coat against two is often the whole gap between two bids.
    </li>
    <li>No drywall repair allowance. Without one, every crack becomes a negotiation.</li>
    <li>Ordering paint by the job total instead of by color, then running short on one of them.</li>
    <li>
      Not asking when the house was built. In homes built before 1978, the EPA’s Renovation, Repair
      and Painting (RRP) rule can require a certified firm and lead-safe work practices, which
      change the prep and the price.
    </li>
    <li>
      No valid-until date. Paint prices move, and an open-ended estimate can be held to old ones.
    </li>
  </ul>
